Branchial Cleft Cyst
Opthalmology & ENT
Lateral neck mass, Anterior to sternocleidomastoid, Non-mobile with swallowing

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-007
Reason for Visit: Acute, painful enlargement of a neck mass with associated fever.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a rapidly enlarging, painful lateral neck mass accompanied by fever following an upper respiratory infection. Urgent physical assessment was critical to differentiate an infected branchial cleft cyst from a rapidly expanding peritonsillar/parapharyngeal abscess or acute suppurative lymphadenitis, preventing potential airway compromise before routine clinical services opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "This lump on my neck blew up overnight. It is throbbing, red, and it hurts when I turn my head."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a previously unnoticed or stable, painless, slow-growing mass in the lateral neck for several years. Over the past 48 hours, following a recent viral upper respiratory tract infection, the mass exhibited a sudden onset of acute localized pain, rapid enlargement, and severe tenderness. Notes overlying erythema. Denies shortness of breath, stridor, drooling, or severe trismus.
Functional Status: Independent, but experiencing localized dysphagia due to pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Nil significant; partner available to assist.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/75 | HR: 98 | Temp: 38.4 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, holding neck rigidly slightly flexed to the affected side.
Physical Exam: A 4cm by 3cm fluctuant, tense, profoundly tender mass is visibly located in the left lateral neck. Anatomically, it is positioned directly anterior to the upper third of the sternocleidomastoid muscle. The mass exhibits significant overlying erythema and localized warmth. It is non-mobile during voluntary swallowing or tongue protrusion. Oropharynx is clear, no tonsillar asymmetry, uvula midline. No stridor or airway deviation.
Environmental Observations: Standard living environment, no hazards noted.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Taking OTC paracetamol with minimal relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Infected Branchial Cleft Cyst - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Thyroglossal Duct Cyst - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lateral anatomical position (anterior to SCM) rather than midline, and lack of vertical movement with tongue protrusion or swallowing.

Parapharyngeal Abscess - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of medial displacement of the lateral pharyngeal wall, absence of severe trismus, and a normal, patent airway.

Acute Suppurative Cervical Lymphadenitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinct anatomical location characteristic of the second branchial cleft, the sheer size and fluctuance of the solitary cyst, and the history of a pre-existing, long-standing painless lump.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a rapidly expanding, acutely painful lateral neck mass and fever, raising immediate concern for deep neck space infection or impending airway compromise. Urgent face-to-face assessment confirmed an acutely infected branchial cleft cyst based on its pathognomonic anatomical location (anterior to the upper third of the SCM) and absence of movement with swallowing. Crucially, the examination definitively ruled out immediate airway threat or parapharyngeal extension. Delaying assessment would have risked undiagnosed abscess rupture or airway compromise. Immediate initiation of high-dose empirical oral antibiotics and analgesia was required to halt progression overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Oral Amoxicillin/Clavulanate 875/125mg BD for 7 days. Oral Ibuprofen 400mg TDS for acute inflammation and analgesia. Arranged urgent outpatient cervical ultrasound.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage localized pain and encourage spontaneous drainage if a sinus tract forms, apply a warm, moist compress strictly for 15 minutes four times daily. The immediate soothing effect of the heat will provide positive reinforcement, ensuring the patient actively manages the localized inflammation while the antibiotics take systemic effect.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: GP review in 48 hours to assess response to antibiotics; refer to ENT for definitive elective surgical excision once the acute infection resolves.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops any difficulty breathing, a change in voice, inability to swallow saliva, rapid massive swelling, or severe rigors.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis requires eventual confirmation via cervical ultrasound and ENT review.
Clinical Course: The acute infection should begin to respond to antibiotics within 48 hours. Once the infection has completely resolved, the cyst will likely remain and require surgical removal to prevent recurrence.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If you start struggling to breathe, your voice becomes muffled, or you cannot swallow your own spit, call 000 or go to the nearest emergency department immediately.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A benign cyst you were born with has become acutely infected following your recent cold. The antibiotics will treat the bacterial infection, and the anti-inflammatories will reduce the painful swelling.
Actionable Steps: Take the antibiotics strictly as prescribed. Apply warm compresses to the area. Do not attempt to squeeze or pop the mass under any circumstances.

